"Benchmarking Optimizersfor Large Language Model Pretraining (https://arxiv.org/html/2509.01440v1)\nciteturn29796view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2509.01440v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 2016\nL0: ##### Report GitHub Issue\nL1: \nL2: [Button: ×]\nL3: \nL4: Title: [Input: Enter title]\nL5: \nL6: Content selection saved. Describe the issue below:\nL7: \nL8: Description:\nL9: \nL10: [Button: Submit without GitHub] [Button: Submit in GitHub]\nL11: \nL12: cite0†Back to arXiv L13: \nL14: cite1†Why HTML?†info.arxiv.org cite2†Report Issue cite3†Back to Abstract cite4†Download PDF L15:   1. cite5†Abstract L16:   2. cite6†1 Introduction L17:   3. cite7†2 Background & Related Work L18:   4. cite8†3 Experimental Setup L19:   5. cite9†4 Results L20:     1. cite10†4.1 Benchmarking & Ablations at Small Scale: Training Models of $\\mathbf{124M}$ Parameters L21:     2. cite11†4.2 Benchmarking & Ablations at Medium Scale: Training Models of $\\mathbf{210M}$ Parameters L22:     3. cite12†4.3 Scaling Up: Benchmarking models of $\\mathbf{583M}$ and $\\mathbf{720M}$ Parameters L23:     4. cite13†4.4 Extension to MoEs L24:   6. cite14†5 Discussion L25:   7. cite15†References L26:   8. cite16†A Optimizers we study L27:     1. cite17†A.1 AdamW, ADOPT, AdEMAMix L28:     2. cite18†A.2 Sign-based methods: Lion and Signum L29:     3. cite19†A.3 Muon & D-Muon, SOAP, Sophia L30:     4. cite20†A.4 Schedule-Free AdamW, Prodigy L31:     5. cite21†A.5 MARS L32:   9. cite22†B Implementation L33:   10. cite23†C Model & Data L34:   11. cite24†D Additional Results L35:     1. cite25†D.1 Ablations for $\\mathbf{124M}$ model L36:     2. cite26†D.2 Ablations for $210\\mathbf{M}$ model L37:     3. cite27†D.3 Wall-clock performance of optimizers across models of different scale L38:   12. cite28†E Hyperparameter tuning L39:     1. cite29†E.1 $\\mathbf{124M}$ parameters model L40:     2. cite30†E.2 $\\mathbf{210M}$ parameters model L41:     3. cite31†E.3 $\\mathbf{583M}$ parameters model L42:     4. cite32†E.4 $\\mathbf{720M}$ parameters model L43:     5. cite33†E.5 $\\mathbf{520M}$ parameters MoE model L44: cite34†License: CC BY-SA 4.0†info.arxiv.org L45: \nL46: arXiv:2509.01440v1 [cs.LG] 01 Sep 2025\nL47: # Benchmarking Optimizers\nL48: for Large Language Model Pretraining\nL49: \nL50: Andrei Semenov Affiliation: EPFL Email: andrii.semenov@epfl.ch    Matteo Pagliardini Affiliation: EPFL Email: matteo.pagliardini@epfl.ch    Martin Jaggi Affiliation: EPFL Email: martin.jaggi@epfl.ch\nL51: ###### Abstract\nL52: The recent development of Large Language Models (LLMs) has been accompanied by an effervescence of novel ideas and methods to better optimize the loss of deep learning models. Claims from those methods are myriad: from faster convergence to removing reliance on certain hyperparameters. However, the diverse experimental protocols used to validate these claims make direct comparisons between methods challenging.\nL53: This study presents a comprehensive evaluation of recent optimization techniques across standardized LLM pretraining scenarios, systematically varying model size, batch size, and training duration. Through careful tuning of each method, we provide guidance to practitioners on which optimizer is best suited for each scenario. For researchers, our work highlights promising directions for future optimization research.\nL54: Finally, by releasing our code and making all experiments fully reproducible, we hope our efforts can help the development and rigorous benchmarking of future methods.\nL55: cite35†https://github.com/epfml/llm-optimizer-benchmark†github.com L56: ## 1 Introduction\nL57: \nL58: Over the past five years, Large Language Models (LLMs) [cite36†24 , cite37†95 , cite38†40 , cite39†82 ] have shown growth in performance and size, demonstrating proficiency in various downstream tasks [cite40†128 , cite41†13 , cite42†145 ]. The success of LLM pretraining hinges on three key pillars: high-quality data [cite43†102 , cite44†77 ], architectural innovations [cite45†57 , cite36†24 ], and scalable optimization techniques [cite46†56 , cite47†122 , cite48†16 ].\nL59: Among these, the choice of optimizer has remained notably consistent in recent years, with Adam(W) [cite49†66 , cite50†84 ] dominating deep learning for nearly a decade. However, recent advances [cite51†59 , cite52†81 , cite53†141 , cite54†99 , cite55†104 , cite56†36 , cite57†27 ] challenge this status quo, offering alternatives that surpass AdamW in speed, communication efficiency [cite58†1 ] or final downstream performance on various benchmarks [cite59†20 , cite60†64 ], particularly for autoregressive language modeling [cite61†110 ].\nL60: Despite these innovations, current benchmarks and ablation studies [cite62†161 , cite63†91 , cite64†60 ] remain narrow in scope, often examining only isolated aspects of optimizer design [cite65†65 ]. This lack of systematic comparison makes it difficult to obtain trustworthy insights for practitioners or identify the next promising research directions.\nL61: Figure 1: Ranking of optimizers for $\\mathbf{720M}$ Llama-based models. We plot the final validation loss obtained by the best-tuned optimizers on the FineWeb dataset. We use a batch size of $1\\mathbf{M}$ tokens and train multiple methods beyond and below the Chinchilla optimal duration, which is $14.4\\mathbf{B}$ for model of this size. AdEMAMix and MARS are the best optimizers in this setup, with a noticable gap in performance compared to other methods.\nL62: We also plot the AdamW baseline in both figures to distinguish the group of methods that consistently perform worse than AdamW from the group of optimizers that outperform it for some training durations. See § cite8†3 and cite28†Appendix E for a detailed description of our experimental setup, including hyperparameters.\nL63: In this work, our goal is to revisit the problem of benchmarking optimizers for LLM pretraining. We do so through standardized experiments which vary important parameters such as batch size, model size, and the number of training iterations. This allows us to formulate an up-to-date list of best-performing methods for the community of researchers and practitioners. We demonstrate the efficiency of each considered method through careful tuning, and present insightful ablations along the way.\nL64: Furthermore, we provide a set of best practices for LLM pretraining that are applicable regardless of the optimizer chosen.\nL65: We summarize our contributions as follows:\nL66: \nL67: (Contribution 1) We conduct the first large-scale, controlled benchmark of $11$ different optimization methods across diverse LLM training scenarios. A fair comparison is ensured by precise accounting for compute costs, and extensive hyperparameter tuning. We identify optimal optimizer choices in several relevant training regimes, for both dense and Mixture of Experts (MoE) architectures.\nL68: (Contribution 2) We perform comprehensive ablations of critical training hyperparameters—including warmup duration, initialization schemes, gradient clipping, final learning rates, and learning rate scheduler choices—providing actionable insights for optimizing LLM training in practice.\nL69: \nL70: (Contribution 3) We open-source our full benchmarking toolkit, including training scripts,\nL71: Figure 2: Training dynamics of leading optimizers on $\\mathbf{520M}$ MoE model pretraining. We use a batch size of $131\\mathbf{k}$ tokens, and train models for both short runs, i.e., less than Chinchilla optimal duration, and for extended runs beyond this regime. The dashed blue lines correspond to the final validation loss of AdamW baselines trained for both $42\\mathbf{k}$ and $336\\mathbf{k}$ steps.\nL72: evaluation pipelines, and hyperparameter configurations, to enable reproducible research and facilitate future optimizer development.\nL73: \nL74: For practitioners, our work provides an evidence-based answer to the burning question: “Is Adam still the most effective optimizer in the age of LLMs, or can we achieve better performance at scale with novel optimizers?”.\nL75: For researchers, our work delivers a unified benchmarking framework for LLM pretraining, along with extensive ablation studies which systematically evaluate both popular and overlooked optimizer designs—revealing previously unexplored tradeoffs between efficiency, stability, and final model performance. Overall, our findings not only challenge long-held assumptions about optimizer selection but also establish a foundation for future advances in large-scale model training.\nL76: By bridging the gap between theoretical innovation and practical deployment, this work aims to accelerate progress in both research and industry applications of LLM training.\nL77: ## 2 Background & Related Work\nL78: Optimizers. While computer vision models often show comparable performance between SGD [cite66†117 ] and AdamW [cite67†159 ], the landscape differs dramatically in LLM training [cite68†130 ]. Recent work [cite69†160 ] demonstrates that adaptive methods like AdamW provide substantially better optimization characteristics for transformer-based language models. The question of why AdamW works so well has been a long-standing topic of research [cite70†5 , cite71†96 , cite72†158 , cite73†74 , cite74†72 ].\nL79: Modern methods often inherit AdamW’s core ideas in their structure, such as ADOPT [cite75†134 ] and AdEMAMix [cite54†99 ]. ADOPT has been motivated by solving long-standing convergence issues in AdamW. By normalizing the second-order moment prior to the momentum update, they eliminate the non-convergence issues of AdamW on smooth non-convex functions.\nL80: Meanwhile AdEMAMix extends AdamW with an additional slower momentum buffer, i.e., a slower exponential moving average (EMA), which allows the use of much larger momentum values, accelerating convergence.\nL81: One interpretation of AdamW’s effectiveness lies in its sign-based update [cite76†73 ]: without the exponential moving average (EMA), AdamW resembles signSGD [cite77†9 ]. Recent works [cite62†161 , cite78†63 ] has shown that Signum (signSGD with momentum), can perform comparably to AdamW. The community also discussed Lion [cite79†17 ], a method with a similar sign-based structure. Signum and Lion offer memory benefits due to the use of only a single instead of Adam’s two buffers for optimizer states.\nL82: Another family of methods stems from AdamW’s approximate second-order structure. This idea has given rise to Sophia [cite80†79 ], where the diagonal of the Fisher information matrix is used as the second moment estimate. Exploiting the matrix structure of model weights and optimizer states has led to methods such as SOAP [cite53†141 ], Muon [cite51†59 ] and Scion [cite55†104 ], including their extentions [cite52†81 , cite81†116 , cite58†1 ].\nL83: The parameter-free concept [cite82†97 ] has led to the development of Schedule-Free AdamW (SF-AdamW) [cite57†27 ] and Prodigy [cite83†90 ]. These optimizers do not require a decreasing learning rate schedule, making them relevant for continual training. Last but not least, MARS [cite84†153 ], builds upon this line of research and incorporates a variance reduction mechanism in its update rule.\nL84: \nL85: cite85†Image: Refer to caption cite86†Image: Refer to caption cite87†Image: Refer to caption L86: \nL87: (a) Batch size $32\\times 512$ tokens.\nL88: (b) Batch size $256\\times 512$ tokens.\nL89: Figure 3: Comparing optimizers for training a $\\mathbf{124M}$ parameter LLM. We plot the validation loss dynamics obtained by considered optimizers. In (a), we train methods with a “small” batch size of $16\\mathbf{k}$ tokens for $\\{64,128,256,384,512,1024\\}\\mathbf{k}$ iterations. In (b), we train methods with nearly $8\\times$ larger batch size of $131\\mathbf{k}$ tokens for $\\{8,16,32,48,64,128\\}\\mathbf{k}$ iterations.\nL90: Thus, in both settings, we result in the same number of tokens models see during the training: $\\{1,2.1,4.2,6.3,8.4,16.8\\}\\mathbf{B}$. We observe that: (I) many methods outperform AdamW in the short runs for $1\\mathbf{B}$ or $2.1\\mathbf{B}$ tokens; (II) as training on more tokens, AdamW narrows the gap with SOAP and D-Muon, while AdEMAMix emerges as the best-performing method; (III) Signum, MARS, Lion, Prodigy benefit from the increased batch size.\nL91: Benchmarks. To a large extent, the benchmarking setup determines the final conclusions. Some benchmarks are designed for short speedruns in terms of training or validation loss [cite88†58 ], while others focus on a downstream target metric after training [cite62†161 , cite59†20 , cite89†121 ]. Methods that perform well in short speedruns might not be optimal for longer training horizons as in real LLM training runs (see cite90†Figure 5 (a), or cite91†Figures 14 and cite92†17 (b)).\nL92: ”But what constitutes a sufficiently long horizon?” ”What should be the compute budget for LLM training?” These are questions explored by scaling laws [cite93†62 ]. Early benchmarks for optimizers and other ablation studies often rely on Chinchilla scaling laws [cite94†51 ] with a ratio of roughly $20$ tokens per parameter needed for pretraining. However, recent research [cite95†75 , cite96†107 , cite97†119 ] argues that this is far from sufficient for production-ready models.\nL93: Another important issue is the choice of loss function. Recent setups have used an auxiliary $z$-loss [cite98†151 , cite99†19 ] in addition to cross-entropy, which requires further investigation. We believe that this choice is influenced by the use of the OLMo [cite100†136 ] codebase, which we also address in our work.\nL94: Additionally, we found that previous setups for comparing optimizers do not align with recent best practices regarding weight decay, learning rate decay, and overall hyperparameter tuning. All of these questions are revisited in our work.\nL95: ## 3 Experimental Setup\nL96: \nL97: Notations. We use the following notations. Let $\\gamma$ be the learning rate, $\\lambda$ the weight decay coefficient, and $T$ the total number of iterations. Momentum-related parameters are represented by the symbol $\\beta$.\nL98: Optimizers. Here is a list of the optimizers we considered in our work.\nL99: For each algorithm, we write in parentheses the optimizer-specific hyperparameters we tuned: AdamW($\\beta_{1}$, $\\beta_{2}$), ADOPT($\\beta_{1}$, $\\beta_{2}$), AdEMAMix($\\beta_{1}$, $\\beta_{2}$, $\\beta_{3}$, $\\alpha$), Lion($\\beta_{1}$, $\\beta_{2}$), Signum($\\beta$), Muon($\\gamma^{\\texttt{M}}$, $\\beta$, $\\beta_{1}$, $\\beta_{2}$), D-Muon($\\beta$, $\\beta_{1}$, $\\beta_{2}$) [cite52†81 ], SOAP($\\beta_{1}$, $\\beta_{2}$) and preconditioning frequency, Sophia($\\rho$, $\\beta_{1}$, $\\beta_{2}$), SF-AdamW($\\beta_{1}$, $\\beta_{2}$), Prodigy($\\beta_{1}$, $\\beta_{2}$), MARS($\\eta$, $\\beta_{1}$, $\\beta_{2}$).\nL100: When an optimizer has several momentum variants e.g. Nesterov [cite101†94 ] or Polyak [cite102†105 ], we try both. When optimizers use the Newton-Schulz orthogonalization [cite103†8 , cite104†49 ], we vary the number of steps for this procedure. In addition, we tune the learning rate $\\gamma$ extensively for all methods. We also try different gradient clipping levels, warmup steps, weight decay values, weights initialization, and learning rate schedulers.\nL101: A summary of the hyperparameters tested and selected for each model size is in cite28†Appendix E . All optimizers are described in depth in cite16†Appendix A .\nL102: (a) Llama $124\\mathbf{M}$ parameters.\nL103: \nL104: (b) Llama $210\\mathbf{M}$ parameters.\nL105: \nL106: (c) Llama $720\\mathbf{M}$ parameters.\nL107: Figure 4: Weight decay in Muon & D-Muon. We compare two methods—basic Muon [cite51†59 ], and D-Muon [cite52†81 ] with a weight decay applied to all parameter groups. Across model sizes  used in our benchmarking of dense LLMs, we observe a major improvement of D-Muon over Muon. We relate this observation to our ablation on the importance of weight decay across different optimizers, and training horizons. See cite105†Figure 8 and cite25†Section D.1 for more details.\nL108: Models & Data. For most experiments, we use a Llama-like transformer [cite39†82 ] architecture with weight tying [cite106†108 ], including SwiGLU activations [cite107†124 ], RMSNorm [cite108†156 ], and RoPE embeddings [cite109†132 ]. We experiment with four sizes of models: $124\\mathbf{M}$, $210\\mathbf{M}$, $583\\mathbf{M}$, $720\\mathbf{M}$. In addition to our dense models, we also benchmark optimizers on a Llama-based $520\\mathbf{M}$ MoE model, the corresponding setup is described in § cite13†4.4 and cite28†Appendix E .\nL109: We train on a $100\\mathbf{B}$ tokens^{1}^{1} 1 cite110†https://huggingface.co/datasets/HuggingFaceFW/fineweb†huggingface.co subset of FineWeb [cite111†101 ]. It consists of a cleaned and deduplicated corpus for LLM pretraining, which we tokenize using the GPT-$2$ tokenizer prior to splitting into train and validation sequences.\nL110: Iterations & Batch size. Throughout our experiments, we use a sequence length of $512$ tokens. For clarity, we often report the batch size in tokens by writing $\\text{{batch size}}\\times\\text{{sequence length}}$.\nL111: For the $124\\mathbf{M}$ model, we use batch sizes of $32\\times 512=16\\mathbf{k}$, $256\\times 512=131\\mathbf{k}$, and $512\\times 512=262\\mathbf{k}$ tokens; for the $210\\mathbf{M}$ model and $520\\mathbf{M}$ MoE model, we use a batch size of $256\\times 512=131\\mathbf{k}$; for the $583\\mathbf{M}$ model, we leverage the batch size of $3936\\times 512=2\\mathbf{M}$ tokens, finally, we use a batch size of $1984\\times 512=1\\mathbf{M}$ tokens for the $720\\mathbf{M}$ model.\nL112: Depending on the model size, we vary the number of iterations—also measured in tokens for compatibility with scaling laws and to accommodate different batch size settings. We train $124\\mathbf{M}$ and $210\\mathbf{M}$ models for equal durations of $\\{1,2.1,4.2,6.3,8.4,16.8\\}\\mathbf{B}$ tokens. This corresponds to $T\\in\\{64,128,256,384,512,1024\\}\\mathbf{k}$ iterations for a batch size of $32$, and $T\\in\\{8,16,32,48,64,128\\}\\mathbf{k}$ iterations for a batch size of $256$.\nL113: For $583\\mathbf{M}$ models, we train on $13\\mathbf{B}$ tokens, corresponding to $6.5\\mathbf{k}$ iterations. In the setup with $720\\mathbf{M}$ model, we have $T\\in\\{8,16,48\\}\\mathbf{k}$ iterations for a batch size of $1\\mathbf{M}$ tokens. Thus, for all model scales, we include both Chinchilla optimal lengths of training and beyond. More details are available in cite23†Appendix C .\nL114: Loss. We train using the classical cross-entropy next token prediction loss. Some prior works introducing optimizers [cite53†141 ], benchmarkings [cite62†161 ], or pretraining recipes for LLMs [cite46†56 , cite99†19 , cite98†151 , cite112†12 ], use a $z$-loss regularizer in addition to cross-entropy. We found that this has little impact and, therefore, do not use $z$-loss. An ablation showing results with and without $z$-loss is in § cite9†4 .\nL115: Hyperparameter Tuning. Training LLMs is a computationally intensive task [cite113†34 ]. As a guidance, practitioners often rely on insights gathered at lower scales, scaling laws [cite37†95 , cite114†23 , cite97†119 , cite115†76 ], and other rules [cite116†152 , cite117†29 , cite118†11 , cite119†71 ]. It is also commonplace to run experiments for only a shorter duration of training, as a way to test certain hyperparameters prior to extending the training horizon to more iterations.\nL116: Because a full grid search over every hyperparameter, for each setting and optimizer, would be too costly, we resort to a similar approach. More precisely, for each model size, batch size, and optimizer, we extensively tune optimization hyperparameters for a number of training tokens which are near-Chinchilla optimal, e.g., we pick $\\{2.1,16\\}\\mathbf{B}$ tokens for tuning $\\{124,720\\}\\mathbf{M}$ models (see cite28†Appendix E ). We then keep those hyperparameters when we increase the number of iterations.\nL117: While we found that the sensitivity to several hyperparameters can change as we increase the training horizon—see cite120†Figure 21 —we found this approach simple and yet effective. The hyperparameters being considered depend on the optimizer. We proceeded from small to large model scale, and used insights gathered at smaller scales to guide the hyperparameter search at larger scales. Our hyperparameter sweeps are summarized in cite28†Appendix E .\nL118: We present the clarifications regarding the connection between the number of iterations and tokens for different batch size settings, as well as the Chinchilla optimal training durations for our models in Tables cite121†3 , cite122†4 , cite123†5 , cite124†6 , and cite125†48 . As learning rate schedulers, we compare cosine [cite126†83 ], linear and warmup-stable-decay (WSD) [cite127†52 , cite128†155 , cite129†54 ]. Unless specified, we use a cosine scheduler. Results with WSD and linear schedulers are discussed in § cite9†4 .\nL119: Recent works also emphasize the importance of sufficiently decaying the learning rate [cite130†7 , cite131†120 , cite129†54 , cite115†76 , cite36†24 ]. As such, we take care to decay to $0.01\\times\\gamma_{\\max}$ instead of the often used $0.1\\times\\gamma_{\\max}$ [cite94†51 , cite132†140 , cite133†10 , cite134†146 , cite100†136 , cite135†137 , cite62†161 ].\nL120: To give an idea of how much effort was put into tuning each method, across all model sizes, batches and iterations, we trained a total of $2900$ models, and have spent roughly $30000$ GPU hours. See more details in cite22†Appendices B and cite28†E .\nL121: ## 4 Results\nL122: \nL123: We structure our story starting with smaller models and batch sizes, and gradually scaling up to larger configurations. In some instances, we complement the core benchmarking results with additional ablations and possible best-practices.\nL124: ### 4.1 Benchmarking & Ablations at Small Scale: Training Models of $\\mathbf{124M}$ Parameters\nL125: Results with “small” batches. We first report results when using batches of $32\\times 512$ tokens in Figures cite90†5 (a) and cite136†3 (a). We tune the hyperparameters by training for $2.1\\mathbf{B}$ tokens ($128\\mathbf{k}$ iterations) and then keep those hyperparameters for all other training durations. The best hyperparameters are reported in cite29†Section E.1 .\nL126: We observe how, for the smallest number of iterations we considered ($1\\mathbf{B}$ tokens $\\equiv$ $64\\mathbf{k}$), SOAP, ADOPT, AdEMAMix, D-Muon, Prodigy, and SF-AdamW all outperform AdamW, with D-Muon being the best. As we increase the number of iterations, AdEMAMix takes the lead while AdamW becomes a second, and closes the gap with D-Muon and SOAP. A sign-based methods such as Lion and Signum are expected to perform poorly when the batch size is small.\nL127: Intuitively, this is due to the $\\text{sign}(\\cdot)$ operator being sensitive to gradient noise [cite137†139 , cite138†67 ]. As described in its original paper, MARS also performs poorly when the batch size is small. We found Prodigy, the basic Muon (see cite139†Figures 4 and cite105†8 (a)) and SF-AdamW to underperform in this setting compared to AdamW. On this scale, Prodigy suffers from the lack of bias correction of the learning rate, as well as being sensitive to $(\\beta_{1},\\beta_{2})$ (see cite140†Figure 35 ).\nL128: Importantly, when the batch size is sufficiently small, we observe that Sophia diverges when increasing the number of iterations, even if decreasing the learning rate (see cite141†Figure 32 ). Thus, we decided not to include Sophia at this stage of our benchmarking.\nL129: Results with “large” batches. We now report results when using batches of $256\\times 512$ tokens—$8\\times$ larger than for our “small” batch setting. Results in Figures cite90†5 (b) and cite136†3 (b) show how Signum, MARS, Lion, Prodigy greatly benefit from the increased batch size. Remarkably, we observe that the Prodigy method scales similarly to AdamW.\nL130: We emphasize the possible community interest in this algorithm, as its effective learning rate—determined by two EMA sequences—emulates the learning rate behavior of AdamW. When the scheduler is applied and $\\gamma_{\\max}$ of Prodigy is set to $1$ (its default value), these EMAs result in the maximal effective learning rate, which closely matches that of AdamW—see cite142†Figure 40 . For a small number of iterations (e.g.\nL131: $T\\in\\{8\\mathbf{k},16\\mathbf{k}\\}$ corresponding to $1\\mathbf{B}$ and $2\\mathbf{B}$ tokens), all methods outperform AdamW except for SF-AdamW and Sophia. As we increase the number of iterations ADOPT, D-Muon, SOAP, and AdEMAMix take the lead. In particular, AdEMAMix has a consistent lead over other methods.\nL132: While we anticipated—in accordance with Vyas et al. [cite53†141 ]—that SOAP would greatly benefit from the larger batch size, its behavior remains relatively consistent compared to our previous small batch setting.\nL133: (a) Batch size $32\\times 512$ tokens.\nL134: \nL135: (b) Batch size $256\\times 512$ tokens.\nL136: Figure 5: Ranking of optimizers for $\\mathbf{124}\\textbf{M}$ models with “small” and “large” batch sizes. In both (a) and (b), we show the final validation loss for different training durations, corresponding to different numbers of tokens. Above each token number, we write the number of training iterations corresponding. In (a), we use a “small” batch size of $32\\times 512$ tokens. In (b), we use a larger batch size of $256\\times 512$ tokens.\nL137: Stability across training horizons. As mentioned in § cite8†3 , we tune hyperparameters training on $2.1\\mathbf{B}$ tokens and keep those hyperparameters when extending the training horizon. However, when increasing the length of training or scaling batch size, critical hyperparameters of optimizers such as learning rate, betas might change [cite143†14 ]. Thus, we additionally re-tune the methods for $16.8\\mathbf{B}$ length of training to show the best results.\nL138: We found that previously widely adopted [cite36†24 , cite144†147 , cite62†161 , cite46†56 , cite129†54 , cite115†76 ] for AdamW ($\\beta_{1}=0.9$, $\\beta_{2}=0.95$) parameters give worse results than ($\\beta_{1}=0.9$, $\\beta_{2}=0.999$). We point that it would be beneficial to further increase the $\\beta_{2}$ for AdamW-like optimizers when increasing the length of training.\nL139: The same applies to $\\beta_{3}$ parameter of AdEMAMix, which we increase from $0.999$ to $0.9999$ when training on $16.8\\mathbf{B}$ tokens and beyond (see cite25†Section D.1 for a detailed ablation on that matter and references therein). Importantly, from cite136†Figure 3 (b), we see that SOAP and D-Muon narrow the gap with AdEMAMix. It is interesting to see how the situation changes when the training horizon is extended to $33.6\\mathbf{B}$ tokens ($\\equiv 256\\mathbf{k}$ iterations).\nL140: For this experiment, we use the batch size of ($256\\times 512$), and keep the re-tuned hyperparameters we found for $16.8\\mathbf{B}$ tokens run, simply reusing them for longer training. We report insights gathered from this ablation in cite145†Figure 6 (right). As in the “small” batch ablation, we emphasize that Sophia exhibits convergence issues when extending the training run, and diverges shortly after $130\\mathbf{k}$ steps (cite146†Figure 31 ).\nL141: Regarding other optimizers, we observe a consistent behavior compared to the one from cite90†Figure 5 (b)—all methods remain at the same position in our tier-list. The results suggest that the best hyperparameters found at $16.8\\mathbf{B}$ scale are also consistent w.r.t. doubling the number of steps. “But what can one say about scaling batch size while keeping the same amount of tokens seen?”\nL142: Increasing the batch size further. We also run an experiment with batches of $512\\times 512=262\\mathbf{k}$ tokens, training for $64\\mathbf{k}$ iterations, thus, we keep the total amount of tokens to train on. We show the results of this ablation in cite145†Figure 6 (left). Noticeably MARS becomes the second best-performing method behind AdEMAMix, followed closely by Prodigy, Lion, ADOPT, and SOAP. Interestingly, Signum performs comparably to AdamW.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2509.01455v1] Trusted Uncertainty in Large Language Models: A Unified Framework for Confidence Calibration and Risk-Controlled Refusal (https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.01455v1)\nciteturn29796view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.01455v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 166\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2509.00544v1] Thinking Hard, Going Misaligned: Emergent Misalignment in LLMs (https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.00544v1)\nciteturn29796view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.00544v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 161\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2509.01211v1] Web Fraud Attacks 